# 2. Languages used in NEXUS

Although C is the primary language, using additional languages for the interface and development tools is a sensible engineering decision.

| Language | Role in NEXUS | Why we need it |
|---|---|---|
| C17 | Core database engine and server | Memory management, data structures, file I/O, networking, concurrency and low-level control |
| HTML5 | GUI structure | Defines the SQL editor, navigation panels, tables and dashboard layout |
| CSS3 | GUI styling | Provides a professional interface, responsive layouts, themes and visual styling |
| JavaScript | GUI behavior | Executes queries through the server API, updates results, handles events and renders monitoring data |
| SQL | Database query language | Allows users to create tables, insert records, search data and manage database operations |
| CMake | Build configuration language | Organizes compilation of the growing C codebase |
| Bash or shell scripting | Optional automation | Automates builds, tests, benchmarks and development workflows in Linux environments |

### Why C remains the main language

C will implement the critical systems components:

- Memory allocator.
- Database storage format.
- SQL parser and execution engine.
- B+ tree indexing.
- Buffer pool.
- Transaction manager.
- Write-ahead log.
- Recovery engine.
- TCP server.
- Thread pool.
- Monitoring and diagnostics.

HTML, CSS and JavaScript will provide the user interface. They will not replace the database engine.

Our principle: the core database operations must be implemented in C, not delegated to an existing database library.

We can use standard libraries and appropriate development tools without reinventing every utility. The important distinction is that the central database logic will be your own implementation.

