# 6. Is NEXUS just a copy of an existing database?

This is one of your most important questions.

The individual technologies are not new. The opportunity is to combine them into a system that you implement yourself and can explain in depth.

Consider existing database systems:

| Existing system | What it is known for |
|---|---|
| PostgreSQL | A mature relational database with transactions, indexing, extensibility and sophisticated query processing |
| SQLite | A compact database engine embedded directly into applications |
| MySQL | A widely used relational database for applications and web services |
| Redis | An in-memory data platform supporting multiple data structures and persistence options |

These systems have mature capabilities developed over many years.

We should not claim that implementing a SQL parser, B+ tree, write-ahead log or network server is itself an industry-first innovation.

Instead, NEXUS should distinguish itself through its implementation quality, measurable results, transparent architecture and original engineering decisions.

For example, you could investigate whether your database benefits from a particular page layout, cache replacement policy, indexing strategy or concurrency design. You would then measure the results against a well-defined baseline.

That is how a learning project can develop into meaningful engineering work.

