# 8. How NEXUS could evolve beyond an ordinary database project

Once the core engine is reliable, we can explore more ambitious ideas.

These are future research and development directions, not capabilities we should claim before implementing and testing them.

| Advanced direction | What we would investigate |
|---|---|
| Adaptive buffer pool | Whether workload information can improve cache replacement decisions |
| Query performance advisor | Whether query plans and execution statistics can identify inefficient queries |
| Storage diagnostics | Whether the system can detect and explain common storage inconsistencies |
| Workload simulator | How the database behaves under different read/write ratios and client counts |
| Replication | How committed data can be propagated between nodes |
| Failure injection | How controlled faults affect recovery and availability |
| Cross-platform engine | How much of the database core can remain portable between Windows and Linux |
| Custom benchmarking framework | How alternative algorithms and configurations compare under repeatable workloads |

The strongest differentiator will emerge from a particular problem you investigate, a clear design decision, and convincing evidence that your solution works.

We should aim to develop at least one component that goes beyond a straightforward implementation exercise—for example, a carefully measured improvement to the buffer pool or a particularly well-tested recovery mechanism.

We should not decide that innovation has been achieved until the implementation and evidence support that conclusion.

