# 10. The realistic definition of success

A strong first release should not attempt to compete feature-for-feature with PostgreSQL, MySQL or SQLite.

Instead, define measurable milestones.

NEXUS v0.1 — Working database
- Persistent storage.
- Basic table creation and insertion.
- Basic SQL queries.
- A functional CLI.

NEXUS v0.2 — Storage and indexing
- Page-based storage.
- Indexing.
- Improved query execution.
- Automated tests.

NEXUS v0.3 — Reliability
- Transactions.
- Write-ahead logging.
- Defined recovery guarantees.
- Crash-recovery tests.

NEXUS v0.4 — Server
- Network protocol.
- Multiple clients.
- Concurrency control.
- Authentication and input validation.

NEXUS v0.5 — GUI
- SQL editor.
- Results grid.
- Database explorer.
- Monitoring dashboard.

NEXUS v1.0 — Advanced systems platform
- Query optimization.
- Reproducible performance benchmarks.
- More extensive fault testing.
- Replication and distributed-system capabilities, if completed to a reliable standard.

These are proposed development milestones, not promises about delivery dates.

We can release smaller working versions earlier and publish the implementation evidence as the project grows.

