# My final recommendation

Arul, build NEXUS to demonstrate three things:

1. You understand C beyond syntax: memory, pointers, files, data structures, networking and concurrency.
2. You understand systems beyond application development: database architecture, transactions, recovery, performance and failure handling.
3. You can engineer a complete product: CLI, GUI, testing, documentation, version control and reproducible builds.

The goal is not to claim that you have built something better than every existing database.

The goal is to build something substantial enough that, when an engineer asks you how your storage engine works, how your database recovers from a crash, or how your server handles concurrent requests, you can show the implementation, run the tests and explain the trade-offs.

That is what will make NEXUS a credible flagship project for your portfolio.